          {
            ":db/id": 17592186125697,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186125711,
              ":tutorial/content": "Our general solution for the wolf population in month $t$ is $P(t) = 450 - Ke^{-kt}.$ Now, let's find the specific values of our constants.\n\nWe're told that, in month $6,$ the population was $392.$ So, applying the condition $P(6) = 392,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6) & =392 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-6k} & =392 \\\\ K{e}^{-6k} & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, in month $12,$ the population was $420.$ So, applying the condition $P(12) = 420,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =420 \\\\ 450-K{e}^{-12k} & =420 \\\\ K{e}^{-12k} & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-6k}}{K{e}^{-12k}} & =\\frac{58}{30} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{29}{15} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =58{e}^{6k} \\\\  & =58\\left(\\frac{29}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1682}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wolf population in month $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t) = 450 - \\frac{1682}{15}e^{-(t/6)\\ln(29/15)}.\n$$",
